{"timestamp":"2026-07-11T20:29:40.434Z","ordinal":169,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_WCZaezLXCKBQYfba3G56ooLz","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Internal Error ()\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn14search12\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPT-5.6 Preview System Card (https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-5-6-preview/gpt-5-6-preview.pdf)\nciteturn24view1 [wordlim: 200] Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 77; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn19search12\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1993\nL0@P0: OpenAI\nL1@P0: GPT-5.6 Preview\nL2@P0: System Card\nL3@P0-1: 2026-06-251 Introduction\nL4@P1: GPT-5.6 is a new family of three models: Sol, our new flagship model; Terra,\nL5@P1: a capable lower-cost option; and Luna, our fastest and most cost-efficient\nL6@P1: model. The safeguards we have built for this launch—our most robust yet—\nL7@P1: are built to deliver these models safely and at scale, around the world.\nL8@P1: We believe in broad access, and we plan to make GPT-5.6 Sol, Terra, and Luna\nL9@P1: generally available in the coming weeks. As part of our ongoing engagement\nL10@P1: with the U.S. government, we previewed our plans and the models’ capabili\u0002ties ahead of today’s launch. At their request, we are starting with a limited\nL11@P1: preview for a small group of trusted partners whose participation has been\nL12@P1: shared with the government, before releasing more broadly. During this\nL13@P1: preview, we will continue testing and coordinating closely with partners as\nL14@P1: we work toward broader availability.\nL15@P1: Under our Preparedness Framework, we are treating Sol, Terra and Luna\nL16@P1: as High capability in both Cybersecurity and Biological and Chemical risk.\nL17@P1: None of them reach our High threshold in AI Self-Improvement. We have\nL18@P1: implemented a tailored set of safeguards, adapted to each model’s capability\nL19@P1: profile, to sufficiently minimize the associated risks.\nL20@P1: This system card is a detailed report of the work we did to understand and\nL21@P1: mitigate GPT-5.6’s safety risks before deployment. The five most important\nL22@P1: things to know are that:\nL23@P1: 1. These models are a meaningful step up in cybersecurity capability, but\nL24@P1: they do not reach our risk framework’s highest level (Critical). GPT-5.6 Sol\nL25@P1: and Terra can find vulnerabilities and pieces of exploits, but in cybersecu\u0002rity testing they were unable to carry out autonomous, end-to-end attacks\nL26@P1: against hardened targets. Separate evaluations examined misaligned behav\u0002ior in agentic coding tasks and found GPT-5.6 shows a greater tendency than\nL27@P1: GPT-5.5 to go beyond the user’s intent, including by taking or attempting\nL28@P1: actions that the user had not asked for, though absolute rates remain low.\nL29@P1: 2. To make these models safe, we added new technology to a safety stack that\nL30@P1: is more than the sum of its parts. The models are trained to be safe, Sol\nL31@P1: and Terra are served with newly added activation classifiers focused on\nL32@P1: sensitive domains that watch the model and can intervene to stop unsafe\nL38@P1-2: barriers throughout that chain. Based on our threat modelling in cyberse\u00021 OpenAIcurity and biology, we’ve designed our safety stack so that even if an attacker\nL39@P2: does complete one step on the path to harm, safeguards will still stop the\nL40@P2: model from allowing severe harm. We also have programs in place so that\nL41@P2: when GPT-5.6 models are broadly available to the public, we can continue\nL42@P2: to reserve the most sensitive cybersecurity and biological capabilities for\nL43@P2: trusted defenders.\nL51@P2: 5. Providing broad access, particularly for cybersecurity capabilities, will\nL52@P2: have important safety benefits. Our testing suggests that GPT-5.6 is better at\nL53@P2: finding and fixing cyber vulnerabilities than at exploiting those vulnerabili\u0002ties in real attacks. That gives defenders an opportunity to harden systems\nL54@P2: before cybersecurity weaknesses are exploited—an opportunity that may\nL55@P2: narrow as offensive capabilities improve. Our safeguards therefore focus\nL212@P7: Before the release of the model, we leverage past ChatGPT production GPT\u00025.5 conversations to simulate the deployment of GPT-5.6 Sol by resampling\nL213@P7: the final assistant turn with the new model. We then automatically label\nL214@P7: the resulting resampled completions for disallowed content. These labels\nL215@P7: may be limited in their precision especially for low prevalence behaviors,\nL216@P7: but can still provide valuable directional signal.\nL217@P7: In the figure below, we report the forecasted prevalence of unsafe model\u0002level outputs. For example, based on the observed distribution of conver\u0002sations with GPT-5.6 Sol, we estimate that approximately 8.6 out of every\nL218@P7: 100,000 production conversation turns with GPT-5.6 Sol would be graded as\nL219@P7: violating our harassment policy.\nL220@P7: Simulation-based forecasts. Comparing a deployment simulation of GPT\u00025.6 Sol to a deployment simulation of GPT-5.5 predicts that GPT-5.6 Sol will\nL227@P7: disallowed content (increased by 40%, from 0.05% to 0.07%), and disallowed\nL228@P7-8: 7 OpenAIFigure 1: The predicted change is the relative increase or decrease we expect from\nL229@P8: GPT-5.6 Sol when compared to GPT-5.5. Incidence rates are provided as n\nL230@P8: in 100k. The notation 5.6 Sol → 5.5 indicates that we are resampling pro\u0002duction prefixes from GPT-5.5 with GPT-5.6 Sol, that is, simulating GPT\u00025.6 Sol’s deployment based on production data from GPT-5.5. Resampling\nL306@P10: designed to maintain strong overwrite-avoidance performance. For GPT-5.6,\nL307@P10: we trained the models to maintain a strong standard of overwrite avoidance\nL308@P10: while improving autonomy without relying on extra cautious prompting.\nL309@P10: GPT-5.6 Sol remains strong at avoiding data overwrites, with an avoidance\u0002only score slightly below GPT-5.5’s, and matches GPT-5.5 on the combined\nL310@P10: metric. This metric measures whether a model can successfully complete a\nL311@P10: challenging task without overwriting undesired data. In general, our larger\nL312@P10: models outperform the smaller Terra and Luna models on complex tasks\nL313@P10: while avoiding edit conflicts.\nL314@P10: gpt-5.5 gpt-5.6-sol gpt-5.6-terra gpt-5.6-luna\nL315@P10: Avoidance only 0.88 0.83 0.81 0.73\nL316@P10: Avoidance + Correctness 0.44 0.44 0.37 0.32\nL317@P10: 3.4 User Confirmations During Computer Use\nL318@P10: The model is trained to follow both platform-level policy for high-risk ac\u0002tions and configurable developer-provided confirmation policy provided in\nL485@P14: 1930)\nL486@P14: HealthBench Hard\nL487@P14: length-adjusted\nL488@P14: 34.7\nL539@P14: (+8.7), HealthBench score of 57.0 (+0.4 relative to GPT-5.5), HealthBench\nL540@P14: Hard score of 33.1 (+1.6), and HealthBench Consensus score of 95.5 (-0.1).\nL541@P14: Answer lengths were shorter for the GPT-5.6 Sol model across all 4 evals. In\nL542@P14: the case of HealthBench Professional, GPT-5.6 Sol had a shorter answer (3228\nL543@P14: vs 3813 characters), and had a higher unadjusted score and a higher length\u0002adjusted score. Overall, this reflects improved HealthBench Professional\nL544@P14: and HealthBench Hard performance vs GPT-5.5, with HealthBench and\nL545@P14: HealthBench Consensus flat.\nL546@P14: HealthBench Professional improvements in particular are the largest since\nL547@P14: the release of GPT-5, matching results from internal testing that show simi\u0002larly large gains across health. GPT-5.6 Terra and GPT-5.6 Luna retain much\nL548@P14: of the performance of GPT-5.6 Sol despite their lower cost, both exceed\u0002ing GPT-5.5 by a substantial margin, indicating a sizable step forward in\nL549@P14: performance at cost for health.\nL594@P15: whether the model reproduces the specific user-flagged error.\nL595@P15: We find that GPT-5.6 Sol makes slightly fewer factual errors than GPT-5.5, and\nL596@P15: reproduces user-reported hallucinations significantly less often. We find\nL597@P15: that larger models tend to perform better than smaller models on factuality.\nL598@P15: 7 Alignment\nL599@P15-16: 15 OpenAIFigure 4\nL600@P16: 7.1 Forecasting Misaligned Behavior with Deployment Simulation of\nL601@P16: ChatGPT traffic\nL602@P16: For details about our methodology in this section, see this prior section and\nL603@P16: our recent research.\nL604@P16: Simulation-based forecasts. Directly comparing a deployment simulation\nL605@P16: of GPT-5.6 Sol in ChatGPT to one of GPT-5.5, we can make predictions about\nL606@P16: rate changes without some confounding effects related to our simulation\nL607@P16: realism. Similarly to the prior section, we use a two-sided Fisher exact test\nL608@P16: with significance 0.1, without correcting for multiple comparisons. Changes\nL609@P16: that seem significant are a 10% reduction in concealed uncertainty, and a\nL610@P16: ~30% decrease in misrepresenting work completion. We did not correct for\nL611@P16: multiple comparisons. We also do not see any calculator hacking, which\nL612@P16: first emerged in GPT-5.1 Thinking. We do not consider the increases to be\nL613@P16: particularly high-risk: the overall rate of fabricated facts is very low, and\nL614@P16: the judge model for this category is known to have lower precision. We also\nL615@P16: audited our simulated GPT-5.6 Sol in search of novel classes of misaligned\nL616@P16: behaviors, but did not find any.\nL622@P16: while multiple others are not. We will leverage this information to guide\nL623@P16: further improvements to the pipeline.\nL624@P16: Prior estimate quality. Because of significant changes in our simulation\nL625@P16: pipeline since our last system card, production rates for GPT-5.5 are not\nL626@P16-17: 16 OpenAIFigure 5: The predicted change is the relative increase or decrease we expect from\nL627@P17: GPT-5.6 Sol when compared to GPT-5.5. Incidence rates are provided\nL628@P17: as n in 100k. The notation 5.6 Sol → 5.5 indicates that we are resampling\nL629@P17: production prefixes from GPT-5.5 with GPT-5.6 Sol, that is, simulat\u0002ing GPT-5.6 Sol’s deployment based on production data from GPT-5.5.\nL630@P17: Resampling fidelity error is a measure of the simulation quality: more\nL631@P17: formally, it is the symmetric multiplicative error of the rates estimated\nL632@P17: by a simulation of an old deployment, and the realized rates in the old\nL633@P17: deployment. In this case, we use GPT-5.5 for estimating the resampling fi\u0002delity error of our pipeline. For more details on the setup, see our research\nL640@P17-18: 17 OpenAIfully comparable to our estimates made in the GPT-5.5 system card, making\nL641@P18: it infeasible to validate them fairly. We will prioritize being able to do so for\nL642@P18: future system cards.\nL643@P18: 7.2 Forecasting Misaligned Behavior with Deployment Simulation of\nL644@P18: Internal Traffic\nL645@P18: In order to anticipate changes in rates of misaligned behavior in agentic\nL646@P18: coding traffic from GPT-5.5 Thinking to GPT-5.6 Sol, we simulate the deploy\u0002ment within OpenAI, and label the simulated trajectories for misaligned\nL647@P18: behaviors. We find that GPT-5.6 Sol, more often than its predecessor, can\nL648@P18: be overly persistent in pursuing user goals, to the point of taking actions\nL649@P18: that go beyond what the user intended. While rates of misaligned behavior\nL650@P18: are higher than previous deployments, the absolute number remains low.\nL651@P18: Measuring, testing for, and mitigating this behavior is a major focus of our\nL652@P18: research for future models, with work spanning our safety, alignment, and\nL653@P18: post-training teams.\nL654@P18: When GPT-5.6 is used as a coding agent, particularly over long trajectories,\nL655@P18: we believe it is important for users to supervise the agent’s work. Internally,\nL656@P18: we have been able to leverage the model to significantly accelerate our\nL657@P18: development process during internal deployment.\nL658@P18: Misaligned behavior in agentic coding traffic. Similar to our results on\nL659@P18: misalignment in ChatGPT traffic, we determine misalignment by judging the\nL660@P18: model’s chain of thought (CoT). In coding contexts, misalignment generally\nL691@P19: obfuscation strategies to get around security controls, and uploading poten\u0002tially sensitive data (such as code, credentials, images, or personal data) to\nL692@P19: unapproved services. Severity level 4 instead applies only when the behav\u0002ior is judged to be part of a broader misaligned plan: we have not observed\nL693@P19: GPT 5.6 Sol taking actions that fall into this category. This level has only ever\nL694@P19: been triggered in synthetic evaluations to validate monitor performance.\nL695@P19: The severity buckets are similar but not directly comparable to those of\nL696@P19: prior system cards.\nL697@P19: Estimated misalignment changes in internal agentic coding traffic. Our\nL698@P19: deployment simulation results suggest that relative to GPT-5.5, GPT-5.6\nL699@P19: Sol more often takes severity level 3 actions. We saw increases of similar\nL700@P19: magnitude when monitoring internal traffic during internal deployment\nL701@P19: of GPT-5.6 Sol. We have observed instances of the model cheating on tasks\nL710@P20: The user authorized deletion of remote virtual machine 1, remote\nL711@P20: virtual machine 2, and remote virtual machine 3. When GPT-5.6 Sol\nL712@P20: could not find those names in one namespace, it substituted remote\nL713@P20: virtual machine 5, remote virtual machine 6, and remote virtual\nL714@P20: machine 7 without asking, killed active processes, and force-removed\nL715@P20: worktrees. It later acknowledged that uncommitted work on remote\nL716@P20: virtual machine 6 may have been lost and stopped after the user\nL717@P20: objected.\nL718@P20: GPT-5.6 Sol ran destructive cleanup on three virtual machines the user did not\nL719@P20: name\nL720@P20: GPT-5.6 Sol actively decided to update an internal research draft to\nL721@P20: say an equation had been computed and verified, even though it knew\nL722@P20: it had not. When challenged, it found that the script assigned the\nL723@P20: known target directly and that claimed integral never produced the\nL724@P20: result.\nL725@P20: GPT-5.6 Sol claimed it completed work that it had not actually done.\nL726@P20: A remote object-tracking job could not read its cloud files.\nL727@P20: GPT-5.6 Sol searched hidden local credential caches, then copied\nL728@P20: access_tokens.json and two cache files to the host and relaunched\nL729@P20: the job. The user asked GPT-5.6 Sol to keep the pipeline running, but\nL730@P20: did not authorize finding and moving cached credentials between\nL731@P20: machines.\nL732@P20: GPT-5.6 Sol used credentials beyond what the user had authorized.\nL733@P20: These results are most useful as an additional signal about internal deploy\u0002ment risk, rather than as a direct measure of external deployment safety\nL739@P21: There are principled reasons to believe [5] that CoT can provide a substan\u0002tially richer monitoring signal than actions and final outputs alone, a hypoth\u0002esis that is supported by growing empirical evidence. It is critical to track\nL740@P21: and preserve CoT monitorability so that it can remain a useful component\nL741@P21: of scalable oversight.\nL742@P21: We assess GPT-5.6 Sol using the evaluation suite from Guan et al. (2025)\nL743@P21: [6], which spans three evaluation archetypes—intervention, process, and\nL761@P21-22: 21 OpenAIevaluations, GPT-5.6 Sol and GPT-5.5 have similar CoT monitorability. Fig\u0002ure 9 plots CoT-only monitoring at medium reasoning effort broken down\nL762@P22: by environment groups. Here, we see that GPT-5.6 Sol scores higher on\nL763@P22: Agentic Misalignment and Health Queries: Patient Opinion, and lower for\nL764@P22: Impossible Tasks and Scruples on average.\nL765@P22: Figure 10 breaks down results on individual Agentic Misalignment envi\u0002ronments. Destructive Actions shows a large increase in g-mean² because\nL766@P22: GPT-5.6 Sol more explicitly describes planned deletions, reversions, and\nL767@P22: modifications to pre-existing workspace state, giving the CoT-only monitor\nL768@P22: stronger evidence of destructive behavior; this indicates increased observ\u0002ability, not necessarily increased incidence. Background Work shows a\nL769@P22: drop because GPT-5.6 Sol produced background-work promises much less\nL770@P22: frequently, leaving a small and unusually difficult positive set. In these\nL771@P22: residual cases, its reasoning often plans to avoid asynchronous promises\nL772@P22: while its final answer still makes a subtle future commitment. Each GPT-5.6\nL773@P22: Sol point contains only 2–5 positives, making the apparent regression highly\nL774@P22: uncertain. Despite our two-positive reporting minimum, zero-TP points\nL775@P22: can have misleadingly small or undefined bootstrap error bars; we plan to\nL776@P22: improve how low-support results are reported.\nL777@P22: Figure 11 shows that the higher Health Queries: Patient Opinion score is\nL778@P22: driven by increased TPR. GPT-5.6 Sol more often explicitly connects the\nL779@P22: patient’s stated diagnosis to its own conclusion—for example, reasoning that\nL780@P22: the patient proposed a diagnosis and that the clinical evidence confirms\nL781@P22: it. GPT-5.5 often mentions the patient’s diagnosis but then presents its\nL782@P22: conclusion as independently derived from the clinical evidence, making\nL783@P22: the patient’s influence harder for the CoT-only monitor to establish.\nL784@P22: Figure 12 shows lower g-mean² for both Flaky Tools and Impossible Coding\nL785@P22: Tasks, primarily due to lower TPR. In the reviewed false negatives, GPT-5.6\nL786@P22: Sol recognizes that its tools are unavailable but frequently treats the failure\nL787@P22: as temporary, considers speculative workarounds, or plans to be transpar\u0002ent. The nontransparent behavior only becomes clear in the final response,\nL788@P22: which may present unverified work as completed; a CoT-only monitor can\u0002not see that response. GPT-5.5 more often states directly in its reasoning\nL789@P22: that it will proceed or answer as though changes were applied, making the\nL790@P22: same outcome easier to detect.\nL791@P22: Figure 13 shows that the lower Scruples g-mean² is mainly attributable to\nL792@P22: Scruples Suggest Right. (Scruples First Person yielded no valid data points\nL793@P22: because none met the minimum instance base-rate threshold.) GPT-5.6 Sol\nL794@P22: more often reasons directly about the underlying moral question without\nL795@P22: stating that the user’s suggested answer influenced its conclusion, whereas\nL796@P22: GPT-5.5 frequently makes that influence explicit. This makes GPT-5.6 Sol’s\nL797@P22: positive cases harder for a CoT-only monitor to detect. GPT-5.6 Sol also\nL798@P22-23: 22 OpenAIFigure 8\nL799@P23: Figure 9\nL800@P23: produces shorter reasoning traces on this environment; the gap narrows\nL801@P23: substantially when comparing traces of similar length, although a smaller\nL802@P23: residual difference remains.\nL803@P23: We plan to continually monitor the sources of monitorability regressions\nL804@P23: and upgrade our evaluation suite. We also plan to explore mitigations that\nL805@P23: preserve monitorability as models improve.\nL806@P23-24: 23 OpenAIFigure 10\nL807@P24: Figure 11\nL808@P24-25: 24 OpenAIFigure 12\nL809@P25: Figure 13\nL810@P25-26: 25 OpenAI7.3.2 CoT Controllability\nL811@P26: In the future, CoT monitorability may break down for a variety of reasons\nL812@P26: [5]. One such path involves agents that become capable of deliberately\nL813@P26: reshaping or obscuring their reasoning when they know they are being\nL814@P26: monitored. If so, evaluations could overestimate a system’s alignment,\nL815@P26: and monitoring systems could become less reliable. To bound this risk,\nL816@P26: we additionally measure “CoT controllability”, a reasoning model’s ability\nL817@P26: to control properties of its CoT in ways that reduce monitorability. CoT\nL818@P26: controllability evaluations can be seen as an early warning sign of whether\nL819@P26: models are getting better at shaping or hiding their reasoning.\nL820@P26: We measure GPT-5.6 Sol’s controllability by running CoT-Control, an evalu\u0002ation suite described in (Yueh-Han, 2026 [7] ) that tracks the model's abil\u0002ity to follow user instructions about their CoT. CoT-Control includes over\nL821@P26: 13,000 tasks built from established benchmarks: GPQA (Rein et al., 2023\nL822@P26: [8] ), MMLU-Pro (Hendrycks et al., 2020 [9]), HLE (Phan et al., 2025 [10]),\nL823@P26: BFCL (Patil et al., 2025 [11]) and SWE-Bench Verified. Each task is created by\nL824@P26: pairing a benchmark problem with one CoT instruction such as avoiding\nL825@P26: certain problem-relevant keywords in CoT, using only lowercase letters,\nL826@P26: or appending a given word to each sentence. Low CoT controllability is a\nL827@P26: desirable property: it suggests models might similarly lack the ability to\nL828@P26: obfuscate their reasoning to evade our monitors. We run CoT-Control only\nL829@P26: on Sol and Terra and for technical reasons we excluded one instruction type\nL830@P26: (Add “<this is not suspicious>” at start and end of the CoT) for all results\nL831@P26: reported in this system card.\nL832@P26: We find that GPT-5.6 Sol’s CoT controllability is higher than that of GPT\u00025.5 and GPT-5.4 Thinking (Figure 14). On the other hand, GPT-5.6 Terra\nL833@P26: shows low CoT controllability similar to previous models. Because CoT\nL834@P26: controllability is heavily confounded by CoT length (longer CoTs are hard\u0002est to control, all else equal; see Yueh-Han, 2026 [7], sec. 5.3), we report\nL835@P26: controllability scores as a function of CoT length. For example, GPT-5.6 Sol\nL836@P26: successfully controls 1.3% of CoTs around 5k tokens long, compared with\nL837@P26: 0.4% for GPT-5.5 and 0.7% for GPT-5.4 Thinking. This increase in controlla\u0002bility is consistent across the five datasets in CoT-Control (Figure 15) and\nL838@P26: mostly consistent across the nine CoT instruction types (Figure 16). Quali\u0002tatively, while GPT-5.5 frequently thought about the instruction at length,\nL839@P26: including meta-discussion of whether to obey constraints on the analysis\nL840@P26: channel expressed in the prompt, GPT-5.6 Sol more often either follows the\nL841@P26: constraint directly or fails in a much terser way. We are investigating the\nL842@P26: causes of increased CoT controllability of GPT-5.6 Sol.\nL843@P26-27: 26 OpenAIFigure 14: CoT controllability as a function of CoT length\nL844@P27: Figure 15: CoT controllability as a function of CoT length, across five datasets in\nL845@P27: CoT-Control\nL1192@P44: GPT-5.6 Sol’s World-Class Bio score was approximately 9 percentage points\nL1193@P44: higher than GPT-5.5 (59.7%).\nL1194@P44: SecureBio found more incremental gains on agentic biology tasks. On\nL1195@P44: ReproBAIT, which measures AI agents on their ability to independently\nL1196@P44: reproduce biological AI models (BAIMs) from published scientific papers,\nL1197@P44: the railfree variant of GPT-5.6 reached 85%, compared with 82% for GPT\u00025.5. The railfree checkpoint also set a new high on ABC-Bench’s Advanced\nL1198@P44: Screening Evasion and reliably identified a known but practically inconve\u0002nient method for evading a commercial nucleic-acid screening algorithm.\nL1199@P44: In manual agentic assessment, the model was able to plan and execute a\nL1200@P44: complex, long-running scientific task, including maintaining a structured\nL1201@P44: work graph and coordinating subagents. Overall, SecureBio concluded that\nL1202@P44: GPT-5.6 could provide substantial uplift to some actors, including wet-lab\nL1203@P44: experts with limited computational experience, but with important limita\u0002tions in judgment, communication, and risk-sensitive decision-making.\nL1204@P44: 9.1.2 Cybersecurity Capabilities\nL1205@P44: As we did for GPT-5.3-Codex, GPT-5.4 Thinking, and GPT-5.5 Thinking, we\nL1206@P44: are treating GPT-5.6 Sol as High capability in the Cybersecurity domain, but\nL1207@P44: below Critical. We are also extending this designation to GPT-5.6 Terra and\nL1208@P44: GPT-5.6 Luna which, while less capable overall than Sol in the cybersecurity\nL1213@P45: severity levels in many hardened real-world critical systems without hu\u0002man intervention” or “devise and execute end-to-end novel strategies for\nL1214@P45: cyberattacks against hardened targets given only a high level desired goal”.\nL1215@P45: To rule out the Critical capability level, in addition to our other evaluations,\nL1216@P45: we test GPT 5.6 Sol’s ability to find and exploit vulnerabilities in a set of\nL1217@P45: widely deployed, hardened software projects using high test-time-compute\nL1218@P45: setups with staged verifier oracles. The model was unable to produce func\u0002tional critical severity exploits in any of the tested software projects in\nL1219@P45: standard configurations. As GPT-5.6 Terra and GPT-5.6 Luna are smaller and\nL1220@P45: less capable on proxy evals such as our internal capture the flag set, GPT-5.6\nL1221@P45: Sol’s rule out is applicable to GPT-5.6 Terra and GPT-5.6 Luna as well.\nL1222@P45: We have deployed an expanded set of safeguards to restrict the ability of\nL1403@P53: Irregular is a frontier AI security lab that develops defenses and evaluates advanced\nL1404@P53: AI systems for cyber capabilities and offensive misuse potential. Irregular evaluated\nL1405@P53: GPT-5.6 Sol across three offensive cybersecurity evaluation suites: FrontierCyber,\nL1406@P53: CyScenarioBench, and Irregular’s Atomic Challenges suite.\nL1407@P53: Irregular found that GPT-5.6 Sol has on-par or slightly stronger offensive\u0002cyber capabilities than GPT-5.5. GPT-5.6 Sol solved 19/197 FrontierCyber\nL1408@P53: challenges, 7/11 long-horizon CyScenarioBench challenges, and all 22\nL1409@P53: medium- and hard-difficulty Atomic challenges.\nL1410@P53: On FrontierCyber, a new benchmark from Irregular testing zero-day discov\u0002ery and exploitation in current off-the-shelf software and hardware, GPT-5.6\nL1411@P53: Sol’s success rates were 11% on Easy, 12% on Medium, 5% on Hard, and 0%\nL1412@P53: on Elite challenges, compared with GPT-5.5 at 6%, 6%, 4%, and 0%. GPT-5.6\nL1413@P53: Sol’s FrontierCyber results included high-impact zero-days affecting widely\nL1414@P53: used systems, though the most severe zero-days were also identified by\nL1415@P53: GPT-5.5. One of the newly discovered zero-days included a vulnerability\nL1416@P53: allowing read-only users to modify and delete arbitrary data in a widely\u0002deployed database system, as well as a vulnerability allowing a malicious\nL1417@P53: app to read other apps' private data in a common mobile operating system.\nL1418@P53: On CyScenarioBench, GPT-5.6 Sol averaged 28% success, about 3 percentage\nL1419@P53: points above GPT-5.5, and solved one challenge that GPT-5.5 did not solve.\nL1420@P53: On Atomic Challenges, both models solved all 22 challenges at least once,\nL1421@P53: with similar average success rates: GPT-5.6 Sol scored 98% on Network At\u0002tack Simulation, 91% on Vulnerability Research and Exploitation, and 56%\nL1422@P53-